-- long-term-bank-loans-non-current-amortised-cost-and-sukuk
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>A loan is recognised at the cash actually received, net of the costs of arranging it, and then carried at amortised cost. The effective interest rate spreads coupons, fees, discounts and premiums over the loan's life as one interest expense, so the carrying amount climbs or falls to the amount repayable.</p></li><li><p>The current/non-current split turns on one question, asked at the reporting date: does the borrower have a right to defer settlement for at least twelve months? Since the 2024 amendments to PSAK 201, only covenants tested on or before that date can take the right away; later covenants are disclosed.</p></li><li><p>A loan leaves the balance sheet when it is repaid, extinguished or modified so much that it is in substance a new loan (the 10 percent test). Sukuk follow their own standard, PSAK 410: a sukuk ijarah is a liability with costs amortised straight-line, and a sukuk mudharabah is temporary syirkah funds, outside liabilities.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report long-term bank loans, non-current portion, of 1,675,895 at 31 December 2024 against 3,467,995 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12; "Daftar" is the printed page). The line is 5.5 percent of total assets (11.2 percent a year earlier). With the current maturities of 1,551,034, PHE owes its banks 3,226,929 on long-term facilities. Alongside sit short-term bank loans (15,775), a shareholder loan from PT Pertamina (Persero) (74,873) and sukuk (43,289).</p><p>After reading this page a student should be able to price and record a borrowing, run it at amortised cost, classify it, account for its modification or early repayment, and tell a sukuk from a bond.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration. Borrowings are financial liabilities under PSAK 109 <em>Instrumen Keuangan</em> (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9).</p><h2>1. What long-term borrowings are</h2><p>A borrowing is non-current when it is not due within twelve months of the reporting date and the entity has a right to defer settlement beyond that.</p><ul><li><p><em>Bonds payable</em> are debt sold to many investors under one trust deed (<em>indenture</em>): secured or unsecured, <em>term</em> or <em>serial</em> (maturing in pieces), callable, convertible, or <em>deep discount</em> (zero-coupon).</p></li><li><p><em>Notes payable</em> are written promises to one lender. A bank term loan is a note in all but name.</p></li><li><p><em>Syndicated loans</em> are one loan agreement funded by many banks through a facility agent. Most of PHE's line is this kind (Note 27, Daftar 5/130, PDF p. 147).</p></li></ul><p>A <em>bullet</em> loan repays all principal at maturity; an <em>amortising</em> loan repays in instalments. A <em>floating-rate</em> loan resets its coupon to a benchmark, such as the Secured Overnight Financing Rate (SOFR), plus a fixed margin. PHE's two corporate facilities are unsecured, priced at Term SOFR plus a margin, and mix semi-annual instalments with bullets (Note 27, PDF pp. 152–157).</p><h2>2. Pricing a borrowing: par, discount and premium</h2><p>A lender pays the present value of what it will receive, discounted at the market rate for that risk. If the stated (coupon) rate equals the market rate, the price equals face value, <em>at par</em>. If the coupon is below the market rate, investors pay less, <em>at a discount</em>; if above, more, <em>at a premium</em>.</p><p>For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary issues a five-year bond with face value 100,000 and a 6 percent annual coupon when the market rate is 7 percent.</p><blockquote><p>PV of principal = 100,000 × 0.71299 = 71,298.62</p><p>PV of interest = 6,000 × 4.10020 = 24,601.18</p><p>Price = 95,899.80; discount = 100,000 − 95,899.80 = 4,100.20</p></blockquote><p><em>0.71299</em> is 1 / 1.07^5 and <em>4.10020</em> is the five-year annuity factor at 7 percent. Run semi-annually for a ten-year bond of face 2,500,000 with an 8 percent coupon and a 10 percent market rate (20 periods at 5 percent), the same calculation uses an annuity factor of 12.46221 and gives a discount of 311,554.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Cash 95,899.80 / Cr Bonds payable 95,899.80</p></blockquote><h2>3. Initial measurement and the effective interest method</h2><p>PSAK 109 recognises a financial liability at fair value, <em>net of transaction costs</em> directly attributable to it, when it is not carried at fair value through profit or loss (para. 5.1.1). Arrangement and legal fees are transaction costs. PHE's policy says the same (Note 3g, Daftar 5/44, PDF p. 61).</p><p>Afterwards the liability is carried at <em>amortised cost</em>: the initial amount, plus interest at the <em>effective interest rate</em> (EIR), less cash paid. The EIR is the rate that discounts the contractual cash flows exactly to the initial carrying amount. It folds the coupon, fees and any discount or premium into one rate, so the cost of borrowing is charged at a constant rate on the carrying amount over the loan's life instead of landing in the year the fee was paid.</p><p>For example (illustration), a subsidiary draws a three-year bullet term loan of 100,000, a slice on the scale of PHE's 940,000 bullet, at a fixed 6 percent paid annually and pays a 1,500 arrangement fee.</p><blockquote><p>Initial carrying amount = 100,000 − 1,500 = 98,500</p><p>EIR solves 98,500 = 6,000 / (1 + r) + 6,000 / (1 + r)^2 + 106,000 / (1 + r)^3, so r = 6.5671%</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 150px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Year</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Opening</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest at EIR</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Coupon paid</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Fee amortised</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Closing</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>98,500.00</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,468.56</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,000.00</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>468.56</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>98,968.56</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>98,968.56</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,499.33</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,000.00</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>499.33</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>99,467.88</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>99,467.88</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,532.12</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,000.00</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>532.12</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>100,000.00</p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Total interest expense = 3 × 6,000 + 1,500 = 19,500</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 50px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Journals</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Drawdown</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash 98,500 / Cr Bank loans 98,500</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Year 1</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Interest expense 6,468.56 / Cr Cash 6,000.00 / Cr Bank loans 468.56</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Year 3 repayment</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Bank loans 100,000 / Cr Cash 100,000</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Interest owed but unpaid at year end is shown separately as accrued interest in current liabilities, not added to the loan (PHE policy, Note 3g, Daftar 5/46, PDF p. 63).</p><p><strong>Floating-rate loans.</strong> When the coupon resets to a benchmark, the cash flows are re-estimated each period and the EIR moves with them (para. B5.4.5). The fee is still spread over the expected life. PHE deducts unamortised arrangement fees of 1,080 and 4,636 from gross principal of 3,232,645 (Note 27), only 0.18 percent of it.</p><p><strong>Accruing between dates.</strong> If a coupon date falls after the year end, the elapsed part is accrued. Three months into a semi-annual period, 3/6 of the coupon and of the discount amortisation is accrued. No cash moves at an accrual, so the credit is Interest payable.</p><h2>4. Borrowings not at face value</h2><p>When a note carries no interest, or interest below market, its face value overstates what was borrowed. The liability is the present value at a market rate, and the gap becomes interest over the term.</p><p><strong>Zero-interest notes.</strong> For example (illustration), a subsidiary buys a compressor by signing a note to pay 50,000 in three years, interest-free, when it could borrow at 8 percent. The compressor's cash price is not known, so the entity uses that estimated market rate, the <em>imputed rate</em>.</p><blockquote><p>Note = 50,000 × 0.79383 = 39,691.61, recorded as Dr Equipment 39,691.61 / Cr Notes payable 39,691.61</p><p>Interest: year 1 = 3,175.33, year 2 = 3,429.36, year 3 = 3,703.70; closing balance 50,000.00</p></blockquote><p>If the asset's fair value is known, it fixes the liability instead, and the implicit rate is backed out from it. Recording the compressor at 50,000 would overstate the asset and hide 10,308.39 of interest inside depreciation.</p><p><strong>Below-market loans.</strong> A shareholder that lends cheaply is partly lending and partly contributing capital. For example (illustration), a parent lends 20,000 for three years at 2 percent when the market rate is 8 percent.</p><blockquote><p>Fair value = 400 × 2.57710 + 20,000 × 0.79383 = 1,030.84 + 15,876.64 = 16,907.48</p><p>Dr Cash 20,000 / Cr Loan from parent 16,907.48 / Cr Equity, capital contribution 3,092.52</p></blockquote><p>The parent gave the 3,092.52 as owner. A below-market loan from the government is a government grant under PSAK 220 (formerly PSAK 61, IAS 20, para. 10A), with the benefit deferred and released to profit. PHE's shareholder loan from Pertamina is interest-bearing: 7,259 of interest on an average balance of 75,581 is 9.6 percent (Note 44h–i, Daftar 5/187, PDF p. 204).</p><h2>5. Current portion and current/non-current classification</h2><p>A liability is current if the entity expects to settle it within its operating cycle, holds it for trading, must settle it within twelve months of the reporting date, or has no right at the reporting date to defer settlement for at least twelve months (PSAK 201, formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1, para. 69). The test is a right that exists at the reporting date. Management's intention to repay early, or to refinance, does not change it.</p><p><strong>The current portion.</strong> An amortising loan is split: instalments due within twelve months are current, the rest non-current. The Company's 2023 facility A2, a 24-month bullet (Note 27, Daftar 5/139, PDF p. 156) drawn for 940,000 in May and October 2023 (PDF p. 157), sat in non-current at 31 December 2023 because it fell due more than twelve months later. A year on, it was due within twelve months. Reconstructed from the facility terms (PHE gives only the totals):</p><blockquote><p>Company syndicated loans, current: 2023 = 400,000 (B1) + 300,000 (accordion) + 122,320 (A1) = 822,320; 2024 = 940,000 (A2) + 122,320 (A1) = 1,062,320</p><p>Company non-current: 2023 = 940,000 + 305,360 = 1,245,360; 2024 = 305,360 − 122,320 = 183,040</p></blockquote><p>That one reclassification explains more than half of the fall in this line, from 3,467,995 to 1,675,895, without a dollar being repaid.</p><p><strong>Refinancing.</strong> A loan due within twelve months stays current even if a refinancing is signed after the reporting date but before the statements are issued (para. 72). It is non-current only if, at the reporting date, the entity already has the right under an existing facility to roll it over for at least twelve months (para. 73).</p><p><strong>Covenants and breach.</strong> A <em>covenant</em> is a condition in the loan agreement, such as a maximum leverage ratio. PHE's two corporate facilities require consolidated EBITDA of at least 3.00 times interest, net debt of at most 2.00 times tangible net worth, and net worth of at least USD 8 billion (Note 27, PDF pp. 153 and 157). If a breach makes the loan repayable on demand at the reporting date, the loan is current, even if the lenders agree after year end not to call it (para. 74). It stays non-current only if the lenders agreed <em>by</em> the reporting date to a grace period ending at least twelve months later (para. 75).</p><p>The 2024 amendments, <em>Non-current Liabilities with Covenants</em>, added which covenants count. Only covenants the entity must meet on or before the reporting date affect classification; covenants tested later do not, but the entity must disclose them and any sign it may struggle to meet them (paras. 72B, 76ZA). PHE adopted the amendment with no significant effect (Note 3b, Daftar 5/30, PDF p. 47). For example (illustration), a 500,000 loan with a leverage covenant of 2.00:</p><table style="min-width: 50px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Situation at 31 December 2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Classification</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Covenant tested at 31 December, ratio 1.60</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Non-current</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Ratio 2.30 at 31 December; waiver with 15 months' grace signed 15 December</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Non-current</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Ratio 2.30 at 31 December; same waiver signed 20 February 2025</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Current; waiver disclosed as a non-adjusting event</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Covenant first tested 30 June 2025</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Non-current, with the covenant disclosed</p></td></tr></tbody></table><h2>6. Modification and extinguishment</h2><p>A financial liability is removed when it is <em>extinguished</em>: paid, cancelled or expired (PSAK 109 para. 3.3.1). The difference between its carrying amount and what is paid goes to profit or loss (para. 3.3.3).</p><p><strong>Early repayment.</strong> For example (illustration), the loan of section 3 is repaid at the end of year 2, at par plus a 1 percent prepayment fee.</p><blockquote><p>Loss = 100,000 + 1,000 − 99,467.88 = 1,532.12</p><p>Dr Bank loans 99,467.88, Dr Loss on extinguishment 1,532.12 / Cr Cash 101,000</p></blockquote><p>The unamortised fee of 532.12 is written off at once. PHE's Note 27 implies an event of this kind: PEP's facility A2 balance is nil at the end of 2024 although it had 84 months to run from 2022, while 21,646 of interest was charged on it during the year (PDF pp. 152 and 155). Long-term repayments in Note 50 (1,643,239) exceed the 1,366,241 due in 2024 by 276,998, close to the 276,800 of PEP's A2 that was non-current at the end of 2023, so that balance appears to have been prepaid. PHE does not describe the repayment.</p><p><strong>Settlement with assets or shares.</strong> When a creditor accepts an asset, the asset is first remeasured to fair value and the rest is a gain on extinguishment. When it accepts shares, ISAK 119 (IFRIC 19) measures the shares at fair value. For example (illustration), a 20,000 loan settled with shares worth 16,000:</p><blockquote><p>Dr Bank loans 20,000 / Cr Share capital and share premium 16,000 / Cr Gain on extinguishment 4,000</p></blockquote><p><strong>Modification.</strong> If the lender changes the terms, PSAK 109 asks whether they are <em>substantially different</em>: whether the present value of the new cash flows, including fees, discounted at the <em>original</em> EIR, differs by at least 10 percent from the present value of the remaining original cash flows, which at the original EIR is the carrying amount (para. B3.3.6). For example (illustration), a 100,000 loan at 6 percent with three years left is renegotiated in two ways.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>A: five years at 5%, fee 500</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>B: principal cut to 85,000, five years at 4%</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PV of new flows at 6%</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>96,287.64</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>77,838.98</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Change</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3.71%</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>22.16%</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Result</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Modification</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Extinguishment</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Accounting</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Remeasure at 6%: 95,787.64; gain 4,212.36; fee 500 reduces the carrying amount</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Derecognise; new loan at fair value at the market rate of 8%: 71,424.79</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Journal</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Bank loans 4,212.36 / Cr Gain on modification 4,212.36; Dr Bank loans 500 / Cr Cash 500</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Bank loans (old) 100,000 / Cr Bank loans (new) 71,424.79 / Cr Gain on extinguishment 28,575.21</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The 10 percent test is a floor, not the whole test. A change that alters the instrument's nature, such as a new currency or an added equity conversion feature, is usually treated as substantially different even below 10 percent (practice, STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED). For example (illustration), in a restructuring that takes route B, Morgan National Bank cuts a 10,500,000 note to 9,000,000 at 8 percent for five years, and the new note at the 15 percent market rate is worth 6,837,779, a gain of 3,662,221. PHE's policy follows PSAK 109 (Note 3g, Daftar 5/47, PDF p. 64).</p><p><strong>Benchmark reform.</strong> When LIBOR ended in 2023, loans moved to SOFR plus a <em>credit adjustment spread</em> (CAS). PSAK 109 lets a change required by the reform, on an economically equivalent basis, be treated as a reset of a floating rate: the EIR is updated and no gain or loss arises (paras. 5.4.7–5.4.9). PHE's project loan for Jambaran-Tiung Biru moved from LIBOR + 2.95 percent to SOFR + CAS + 2.95 percent on Tranche A, and from LIBOR + 2.15 percent to SOFR + CAS + 2.15 percent on Tranche B, after September 2023 (Note 27, Daftar 5/132, PDF p. 149).</p><h2>7. The fair value option and off-balance-sheet financing</h2><p>A borrower may designate a liability at fair value through profit or loss to remove an accounting mismatch (para. 4.2.2). The change in fair value due to the borrower's own credit risk then goes to other comprehensive income (OCI), not profit (para. 5.7.7). Own-credit amounts in OCI are never recycled to profit. For example (illustration, the split is this page's), a note of 500 falls to 480, 15 of it from the issuer's weaker credit and 5 from higher market rates.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Notes payable 20 / Cr OCI 15 / Cr Gain in profit or loss 5</p></blockquote><p>Had the whole fall come from credit risk, all 20 would go to OCI under PSAK 109. PHE carries all its borrowings at amortised cost (Note 3g, PDF p. 61).</p><p><em>Off-balance-sheet financing</em> keeps debt out of the borrower's statements, through unconsolidated project companies or special purpose entities. Owning less than 50 percent does not by itself keep a subsidiary out: under PSAK 110 (formerly PSAK 65, IFRS 10) control decides, whatever the shareholding. A <em>financial guarantee</em> the parent gives a lender is recognised at fair value and then carried at the higher of the expected credit loss and the unamortised initial amount (PSAK 109 para. 4.2.1(c)); a <em>comfort letter</em> that is not a guarantee is not recognised. PIEP gave one in 2017 for its subsidiary M&amp;P's syndicated loan and states it "shall not be construed as acting as a guarantor" (Note 27, Daftar 5/134, PDF p. 151). Because M&amp;P is consolidated, its loan is already in PHE's borrowings; the letter matters only in PIEP's own accounts.</p><h2>8. Sukuk: the issuer's side</h2><p>A <em>sukuk</em> is a sharia security representing an undivided share in assets, in the benefit of assets, or in an investment activity. The holder earns a share of rent or profit rather than interest. Indonesia's issuer accounting sits in PSAK 410 <em>Akuntansi Sukuk</em> (formerly PSAK 110; the new PSAK 110 is consolidation, a renumbering trap) and covers two forms.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Sukuk ijarah</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Sukuk mudharabah</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>What the holder owns</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Benefit of a leased asset; return is rent (<em>ijarah</em> fee)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Share of a business venture; return is a profit share (<em>nisbah</em>)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Initial measurement</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Fair value (nominal adjusted for premium or discount), less transaction costs</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Nominal; transaction costs a separate deferred charge</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Afterwards</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Costs and premium or discount amortised straight-line as sukuk issuance expense; ijarah fee expensed when due</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Costs amortised straight-line; the holders' share is a distribution of revenue, not an expense</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Presentation</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Liability, net of unamortised costs</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Temporary syirkah funds, between liabilities and equity (last among liabilities if the entity has no such section)</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Both forms spread costs straight-line, where PSAK 109 would use the effective interest rate. PHE's own policy lists the sukuk payable among loans and borrowings at amortised cost under the EIR method (Note 3g, PDF pp. 61 and 63), and never cites PSAK 410.</p><p>PHE's sukuk payable is a sukuk ijarah issued by its subsidiary Elnusa on 11 August 2020: Rp700,000 million, five years, an ijarah fee of Rp63,000 million a year (9 percent) paid quarterly, maturing 11 August 2025 (Note 22, Daftar 5/116–5/117, PDF pp. 133–134). For example (illustration), translate it at one constant rate, so nominal 43,325 and issuance costs 224 as in Note 22.</p><blockquote><p>On issue: Dr Cash 43,101 / Cr Sukuk ijarah payable 43,101</p><p>Each quarter: Dr Ijarah expense 974.81 / Cr Cash 974.81, where 974.81 = 43,325 × 9% / 4</p><p>Each year: Dr Sukuk issuance expense 44.80 / Cr Sukuk ijarah payable 44.80, where 44.80 = 224 / 5</p></blockquote><p>Note 22 shows the net: 43,325 − 224 + 188 = 43,289. Islamic tranches also appear inside PHE's bank loans: a <em>wakala</em> (agency-based) tranche of the Jambaran-Tiung Biru financing and a Bank Syariah Indonesia facility to an Elnusa subsidiary. PHE reports both as bank loans; the wakala tranche is priced off SOFR like the conventional tranches (Note 27, PDF pp. 148 and 160).</p><h2>9. Borrowings across industries</h2><p><strong>Banks.</strong> Borrowings, subordinated debt and deposits are all financial liabilities at amortised cost, but banks present the balance sheet in order of liquidity without a current/non-current split, which PSAK 201 allows when it is more relevant (para. 60). Maturity analysis then moves to the notes.</p><p><strong>Power, toll roads and other project finance.</strong> A project company borrows <em>non-recourse</em>: lenders are repaid only from the project's cash flows. Covenants centre on the <em>debt service coverage ratio</em> (DSCR), cash flow available for debt service divided by principal and interest due. For example (illustration), cash available 13,000 against debt service of 10,000 gives 1.30 against a covenant of 1.20. Lenders often require a <em>debt service reserve account</em>, which the borrower shows as restricted cash (see the Restricted cash essay). Interest during construction is capitalised under PSAK 223 (see the Fixed assets essay).</p><p><strong>Mining and plantations.</strong> Many Indonesian commodity exporters borrow in USD because they sell in USD, a natural hedge. Many also have the US dollar as functional currency (PSAK 221, IAS 21), as PHE does, so the loan creates no exchange differences. A company whose functional currency is the rupiah carries the gains and losses through profit unless it designates a hedge.</p><p><strong>State-owned enterprises.</strong> Funds on-lent by the government through <em>subsidiary loan agreements</em> are borrowings. Any below-market element is a grant under PSAK 220 when the government acts as lender or regulator; when it lends as the SOE's shareholder, the logic of a parent's loan (section 4) points to a capital contribution. Which applies is a judgement on the facts.</p><h2>10. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Notes 26, 27, 22, 44h and 50</h2><p>PSAK 107 (IFRS 7) asks for carrying amounts, terms, a maturity analysis, interest, defaults and breaches. Note 27 gives each facility by borrower (Daftar 5/130, PDF p. 147).</p><table style="min-width: 125px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Borrower</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Current 2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Non-current 2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Total 2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Total 2023</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PHE (the Company)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,062,320</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>183,040</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,245,360</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,067,680</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PEPC (Jambaran-Tiung Biru)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>189,212</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,019,207</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,208,419</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,386,663</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PHI</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>166,800</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>249,600</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>416,400</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>583,200</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PEP</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>88,960</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>133,120</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>222,080</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>649,440</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PIEP (M&amp;P)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>39,561</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>64,900</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>104,461</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>146,467</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PDSI and Elnusa (rupiah)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>5,261</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>30,664</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>35,925</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>14,250</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Gross principal</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,552,114</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,680,531</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,232,645</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>4,847,700</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Less arrangement fees</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(1,080)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(4,636)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(5,716)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(14,243)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Long-term bank loans</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>1,551,034</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>1,675,895</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>3,226,929</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>4,833,457</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Note 50 reconciles all borrowings, including sukuk and the shareholder loan (Daftar 5/235, PDF p. 252).</p><blockquote><p>4,978,575 + drawdowns 56,412 − repayments 1,679,292 + non-cash 5,171 = 3,360,866</p><p>= short-term 15,775 + long-term 3,226,929 + shareholder loan 74,873 + sukuk 43,289</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>Share due within one year = 1,551,034 / 3,226,929 = 48.1%</p><p>Implied cost of borrowing = interest on loans 297,328 (Note 41) / average borrowings ((4,978,575 + 3,360,866) / 2 = 4,169,721) = 7.1%, approximate, since the numerator is after 2,277 of interest capitalised (Note 12, PDF p. 126)</p><p>Net debt = 3,360,866 − cash 2,614,318 = 746,548; against total equity 15,107,352 = 0.05</p></blockquote><p>The last ratio is only a proxy for the covenant, whose definitions of net debt and tangible net worth are not disclosed, but it suggests wide headroom against 2.00. PHE states it complied with all covenants (Note 27). Almost half the line falls due in 2025, mainly the 940,000 bullet. PHE discloses no refinancing of it at the reporting date, and it held cash of 2,614,318. Note 27 shows 560,000 undrawn under the 2023 agreement, without saying whether it was still available (PDF p. 157). The USD 200 million six-month loan PHE took in February 2025 (Note 52c, Daftar 5/237, PDF p. 254) came from PAEP, a wholly owned subsidiary (Note 1), so it leaves consolidated borrowings unchanged; an external loan signed then would not have reclassified the bullet either (para. 72).</p><p>Two disclosures do not follow the rules taught above.</p><ul><li><p>The sukuk matures on 11 August 2025, within twelve months, yet it is presented as non-current on the balance sheet and in the 1–5 year column of the liquidity table (Note 49, Daftar 5/233, PDF p. 250). Under section 5 it should be current.</p></li><li><p>The liquidity table's "bank and shareholder loans" due within a year, 1,566,809, equals long-term current maturities plus short-term loans (1,551,034 + 15,775) and leaves out the 74,873 shareholder loan, as does the 2023 column. It also shows carrying amounts, where PSAK 107 asks for undiscounted contractual cash flows.</p></li></ul><h2>11. Upstream note: project finance at Jambaran-Tiung Biru</h2><p>PEPC financed the Jambaran-Tiung Biru gas development through a <em>trustee borrowing structure</em>: facilities of 1,679,940 after a 166,460 cancellation. The 122,859 remaining in the trustee accounts is carried as restricted cash (Note 27, PDF pp. 148–150). Half of each tranche's notional is swapped from the floating benchmark to fixed base rates of 1.79–1.99 percent (315,000 of 630,000; 479,970 of 959,940), so half the notional was hedged at the effective date; the swaps' amortisation profile is not disclosed. Tranches amortise semi-annually to 2029 and 2034, the longest-dated part of the line.</p><h2>12. Related lines</h2><ul><li><p><code>long-term-bank-loans-current</code>, <code>short-term-bank-loans</code>, <code>other-loans-current</code>, <code>sukuk-payables</code>: the rest of PHE's borrowings, covered on this page.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/cash-and-cash-equivalents">cash-and-cash-equivalents</a> and <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/restricted-cash">restricted-cash</a>: the other side of net debt and the trustee account.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/finance-cost">finance-cost</a>: interest at the effective rate.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/fixed-assets-net">fixed-assets-net</a>: capitalised borrowing costs.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/right-of-use-assets-net">lease-liabilities-current</a>: the other interest-bearing debt (see the Right-of-use assets essay).</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>That PEP prepaid about 277,000 of its facility A2 in 2024 is inferred from Note 27 and Note 50; PHE does not describe it, and the 8,527 fall in unamortised fees is consistent with but does not prove a write-off.</p></li><li><p>PHE appears to measure the sukuk ijarah under PSAK 109 rather than PSAK 410. Its policy puts the sukuk among loans at amortised cost by the EIR method, and the amortised costs (83.9 and 61.7 percent of the total at the end of 2024 and 2023) fit an effective-interest pattern (about 82–88 and 60–65 percent) rather than straight line (87.8 and 67.7 percent). With costs of 224 on 43,325 the difference is immaterial. The sukuk is also shown as non-current although it matures in August 2025.</p></li><li><p>The PSAK 410 treatment in section 8 is from an IAI teaching deck that disclaims being IAI's official view. The standard's text was not opened.</p></li><li><p>The covenant proxy of 0.05 uses total equity and reported cash. The agreements' definitions are not disclosed.</p></li><li><p>Other PHE slips not used in the body: Note 27's facility A1 total reads 943,480,000 for 943,840,000 (PDF p. 155); facility interest (299,827 with the shareholder loan) exceeds Note 41's 297,328 by 2,499, mostly the 2,277 capitalised (Note 12), with 222 and the location of the sukuk fee and M&amp;P's interest undisclosed. The industry notes in section 9 are general practice, not checked against named companies.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1–1/3, PDF pp. 11–13); Note 1 (subsidiaries); Note 12 (PDF p. 126); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 3b (Daftar 5/30, PDF p. 47); Note 3g (Daftar 5/44–5/47, PDF pp. 61–64); Note 22 (Daftar 5/116–5/117, PDF pp. 133–134); Note 26 (Daftar 5/125, PDF p. 142); Note 27 (Daftar 5/130–5/143, PDF pp. 147–160); Note 41 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167); Note 44h–i (Daftar 5/187, PDF p. 204); Note 47 (Daftar 5/222–5/224, PDF pp. 239–241); Note 49 liquidity table (Daftar 5/233, PDF p. 250); Note 50 (Daftar 5/235, PDF p. 252); Note 52c (Daftar 5/237, PDF p. 254). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 109 (IFRS 9), paras. 3.3.1, 3.3.3, 4.2.1(c), 4.2.2, 5.1.1, 5.4.7–5.4.9, 5.7.7, B3.3.6, B5.4.5: 10 percent test VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation agenda papers; IFRIC update Nov 2016 via IAS Plus); other paragraphs STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 201 (IAS 1), paras. 60, 69, 72–75; 2022 amendment <em>Non-current Liabilities with Covenants</em>, paras. 72B, 76ZA, effective 1 January 2024: amendment content VERIFIED (web, BDO IFR Bulletin 2022/07; EY Developments 209); paras. 60, 69, 72–75 STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 410 <em>Akuntansi Sukuk</em> (formerly PSAK 110): number VERIFIED (web, IAI, <em>DSAS Terkini</em> XV 2023; Binus Accounting, 2024). Issuer measurement and presentation VERIFIED (web, IAI, "PSAK Syariah for Academics – Akuntansi Sukuk", 28 Feb 2024, slides 36–40, speaker's personal views; fetched again at recheck).</p></li><li><p>ISAK 119 (IFRIC 19), PSAK 220 (IAS 20) para. 10A, PSAK 221 (IAS 21), PSAK 110 (IFRS 10), PSAK 107 (IFRS 7), PSAK 223 (IAS 23): numbers from the IAI renumbering rule (IFRS n → PSAK 100+n, IAS n → PSAK 200+n, IFRIC n → ISAK 100+n); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, <em>Intermediate Accounting</em>, IFRS edition, ch. 14. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A loan is recognised at the cash actually received, net of the costs of arranging it, and then carried at amortised cost. The effective interest rate spreads coupons, fees, discounts and premiums over the loan's life as one interest expense, so the carrying amount climbs or falls to the amount repayable.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The current/non-current split turns on one question, asked at the reporting date: does the borrower have a right to defer settlement for at least twelve months? Since the 2024 amendments to PSAK 201, only covenants tested on or before that date can take the right away; later covenants are disclosed.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A loan leaves the balance sheet when it is repaid, extinguished or modified so much that it is in substance a new loan (the 10 percent test). Sukuk follow their own standard, PSAK 410: a sukuk ijarah is a liability with costs amortised straight-line, and a sukuk mudharabah is temporary syirkah funds, outside liabilities.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report long-term bank loans, non-current portion, of 1,675,895 at 31 December 2024 against 3,467,995 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The line is 5.5 percent of total assets (11.2 percent a year earlier). With the current maturities of 1,551,034, PHE owes its banks 3,226,929 on long-term facilities. Alongside sit short-term bank loans (15,775), a shareholder loan from PT Pertamina (Persero) (74,873) and sukuk (43,289).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After reading this page a student should be able to price and record a borrowing, run it at amortised cost, classify it, account for its modification or early repayment, and tell a sukuk from a bond.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration. Borrowings are financial liabilities under PSAK 109 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Instrumen Keuangan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. What long-term borrowings are","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A borrowing is non-current when it is not due within twelve months of the reporting date and the entity has a right to defer settlement beyond that.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Bonds payable","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" are debt sold to many investors under one trust deed (","type":"text"},{"text":"indenture","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"): secured or unsecured, ","type":"text"},{"text":"term","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" or ","type":"text"},{"text":"serial","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (maturing in pieces), callable, convertible, or ","type":"text"},{"text":"deep discount","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (zero-coupon).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Notes payable","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" are written promises to one lender. A bank term loan is a note in all but name.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Syndicated loans","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" are one loan agreement funded by many banks through a facility agent. Most of PHE's line is this kind (Note 27, Daftar 5/130, PDF p. 147).","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A ","type":"text"},{"text":"bullet","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" loan repays all principal at maturity; an ","type":"text"},{"text":"amortising","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" loan repays in instalments. A ","type":"text"},{"text":"floating-rate","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" loan resets its coupon to a benchmark, such as the Secured Overnight Financing Rate (SOFR), plus a fixed margin. PHE's two corporate facilities are unsecured, priced at Term SOFR plus a margin, and mix semi-annual instalments with bullets (Note 27, PDF pp. 152–157).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. Pricing a borrowing: par, discount and premium","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A lender pays the present value of what it will receive, discounted at the market rate for that risk. If the stated (coupon) rate equals the market rate, the price equals face value, ","type":"text"},{"text":"at par","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". If the coupon is below the market rate, investors pay less, ","type":"text"},{"text":"at a discount","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"; if above, more, ","type":"text"},{"text":"at a premium","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary issues a five-year bond with face value 100,000 and a 6 percent annual coupon when the market rate is 7 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PV of principal = 100,000 × 0.71299 = 71,298.62","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PV of interest = 6,000 × 4.10020 = 24,601.18","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Price = 95,899.80; discount = 100,000 − 95,899.80 = 4,100.20","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0.71299","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is 1 / 1.07^5 and ","type":"text"},{"text":"4.10020","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is the five-year annuity factor at 7 percent. Run semi-annually for a ten-year bond of face 2,500,000 with an 8 percent coupon and a 10 percent market rate (20 periods at 5 percent), the same calculation uses an annuity factor of 12.46221 and gives a discount of 311,554.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 95,899.80 / Cr Bonds payable 95,899.80","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. Initial measurement and the effective interest method","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 109 recognises a financial liability at fair value, ","type":"text"},{"text":"net of transaction costs","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" directly attributable to it, when it is not carried at fair value through profit or loss (para. 5.1.1). Arrangement and legal fees are transaction costs. PHE's policy says the same (Note 3g, Daftar 5/44, PDF p. 61).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Afterwards the liability is carried at ","type":"text"},{"text":"amortised cost","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": the initial amount, plus interest at the ","type":"text"},{"text":"effective interest rate","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (EIR), less cash paid. The EIR is the rate that discounts the contractual cash flows exactly to the initial carrying amount. It folds the coupon, fees and any discount or premium into one rate, so the cost of borrowing is charged at a constant rate on the carrying amount over the loan's life instead of landing in the year the fee was paid.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a subsidiary draws a three-year bullet term loan of 100,000, a slice on the scale of PHE's 940,000 bullet, at a fixed 6 percent paid annually and pays a 1,500 arrangement fee.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Initial carrying amount = 100,000 − 1,500 = 98,500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"EIR solves 98,500 = 6,000 / (1 + r) + 6,000 / (1 + r)^2 + 106,000 / (1 + r)^3, so r = 6.5671%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Opening","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest at EIR","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Coupon paid","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Fee amortised","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Closing","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"98,500.00","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,468.56","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,000.00","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"468.56","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"98,968.56","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"98,968.56","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,499.33","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,000.00","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"499.33","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"99,467.88","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"99,467.88","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,532.12","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,000.00","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"532.12","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"100,000.00","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total interest expense = 3 × 6,000 + 1,500 = 19,500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Journals","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Drawdown","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 98,500 / Cr Bank loans 98,500","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 1","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Interest expense 6,468.56 / Cr Cash 6,000.00 / Cr Bank loans 468.56","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 3 repayment","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Bank loans 100,000 / Cr Cash 100,000","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest owed but unpaid at year end is shown separately as accrued interest in current liabilities, not added to the loan (PHE policy, Note 3g, Daftar 5/46, PDF p. 63).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Floating-rate loans.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" When the coupon resets to a benchmark, the cash flows are re-estimated each period and the EIR moves with them (para. B5.4.5). The fee is still spread over the expected life. PHE deducts unamortised arrangement fees of 1,080 and 4,636 from gross principal of 3,232,645 (Note 27), only 0.18 percent of it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accruing between dates.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" If a coupon date falls after the year end, the elapsed part is accrued. Three months into a semi-annual period, 3/6 of the coupon and of the discount amortisation is accrued. No cash moves at an accrual, so the credit is Interest payable.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Borrowings not at face value","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"When a note carries no interest, or interest below market, its face value overstates what was borrowed. The liability is the present value at a market rate, and the gap becomes interest over the term.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Zero-interest notes.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" For example (illustration), a subsidiary buys a compressor by signing a note to pay 50,000 in three years, interest-free, when it could borrow at 8 percent. The compressor's cash price is not known, so the entity uses that estimated market rate, the ","type":"text"},{"text":"imputed rate","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note = 50,000 × 0.79383 = 39,691.61, recorded as Dr Equipment 39,691.61 / Cr Notes payable 39,691.61","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest: year 1 = 3,175.33, year 2 = 3,429.36, year 3 = 3,703.70; closing balance 50,000.00","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"If the asset's fair value is known, it fixes the liability instead, and the implicit rate is backed out from it. Recording the compressor at 50,000 would overstate the asset and hide 10,308.39 of interest inside depreciation.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Below-market loans.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A shareholder that lends cheaply is partly lending and partly contributing capital. For example (illustration), a parent lends 20,000 for three years at 2 percent when the market rate is 8 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Fair value = 400 × 2.57710 + 20,000 × 0.79383 = 1,030.84 + 15,876.64 = 16,907.48","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 20,000 / Cr Loan from parent 16,907.48 / Cr Equity, capital contribution 3,092.52","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The parent gave the 3,092.52 as owner. A below-market loan from the government is a government grant under PSAK 220 (formerly PSAK 61, IAS 20, para. 10A), with the benefit deferred and released to profit. PHE's shareholder loan from Pertamina is interest-bearing: 7,259 of interest on an average balance of 75,581 is 9.6 percent (Note 44h–i, Daftar 5/187, PDF p. 204).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Current portion and current/non-current classification","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A liability is current if the entity expects to settle it within its operating cycle, holds it for trading, must settle it within twelve months of the reporting date, or has no right at the reporting date to defer settlement for at least twelve months (PSAK 201, formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1, para. 69). The test is a right that exists at the reporting date. Management's intention to repay early, or to refinance, does not change it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The current portion.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" An amortising loan is split: instalments due within twelve months are current, the rest non-current. The Company's 2023 facility A2, a 24-month bullet (Note 27, Daftar 5/139, PDF p. 156) drawn for 940,000 in May and October 2023 (PDF p. 157), sat in non-current at 31 December 2023 because it fell due more than twelve months later. A year on, it was due within twelve months. Reconstructed from the facility terms (PHE gives only the totals):","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Company syndicated loans, current: 2023 = 400,000 (B1) + 300,000 (accordion) + 122,320 (A1) = 822,320; 2024 = 940,000 (A2) + 122,320 (A1) = 1,062,320","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Company non-current: 2023 = 940,000 + 305,360 = 1,245,360; 2024 = 305,360 − 122,320 = 183,040","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That one reclassification explains more than half of the fall in this line, from 3,467,995 to 1,675,895, without a dollar being repaid.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Refinancing.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A loan due within twelve months stays current even if a refinancing is signed after the reporting date but before the statements are issued (para. 72). It is non-current only if, at the reporting date, the entity already has the right under an existing facility to roll it over for at least twelve months (para. 73).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Covenants and breach.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A ","type":"text"},{"text":"covenant","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is a condition in the loan agreement, such as a maximum leverage ratio. PHE's two corporate facilities require consolidated EBITDA of at least 3.00 times interest, net debt of at most 2.00 times tangible net worth, and net worth of at least USD 8 billion (Note 27, PDF pp. 153 and 157). If a breach makes the loan repayable on demand at the reporting date, the loan is current, even if the lenders agree after year end not to call it (para. 74). It stays non-current only if the lenders agreed ","type":"text"},{"text":"by","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" the reporting date to a grace period ending at least twelve months later (para. 75).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 2024 amendments, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Non-current Liabilities with Covenants","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", added which covenants count. Only covenants the entity must meet on or before the reporting date affect classification; covenants tested later do not, but the entity must disclose them and any sign it may struggle to meet them (paras. 72B, 76ZA). PHE adopted the amendment with no significant effect (Note 3b, Daftar 5/30, PDF p. 47). For example (illustration), a 500,000 loan with a leverage covenant of 2.00:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Situation at 31 December 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Classification","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Covenant tested at 31 December, ratio 1.60","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Non-current","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Ratio 2.30 at 31 December; waiver with 15 months' grace signed 15 December","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Non-current","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Ratio 2.30 at 31 December; same waiver signed 20 February 2025","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Current; waiver disclosed as a non-adjusting event","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Covenant first tested 30 June 2025","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Non-current, with the covenant disclosed","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Modification and extinguishment","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A financial liability is removed when it is ","type":"text"},{"text":"extinguished","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": paid, cancelled or expired (PSAK 109 para. 3.3.1). The difference between its carrying amount and what is paid goes to profit or loss (para. 3.3.3).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Early repayment.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" For example (illustration), the loan of section 3 is repaid at the end of year 2, at par plus a 1 percent prepayment fee.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Loss = 100,000 + 1,000 − 99,467.88 = 1,532.12","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Bank loans 99,467.88, Dr Loss on extinguishment 1,532.12 / Cr Cash 101,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The unamortised fee of 532.12 is written off at once. PHE's Note 27 implies an event of this kind: PEP's facility A2 balance is nil at the end of 2024 although it had 84 months to run from 2022, while 21,646 of interest was charged on it during the year (PDF pp. 152 and 155). Long-term repayments in Note 50 (1,643,239) exceed the 1,366,241 due in 2024 by 276,998, close to the 276,800 of PEP's A2 that was non-current at the end of 2023, so that balance appears to have been prepaid. PHE does not describe the repayment.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Settlement with assets or shares.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" When a creditor accepts an asset, the asset is first remeasured to fair value and the rest is a gain on extinguishment. When it accepts shares, ISAK 119 (IFRIC 19) measures the shares at fair value. For example (illustration), a 20,000 loan settled with shares worth 16,000:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Bank loans 20,000 / Cr Share capital and share premium 16,000 / Cr Gain on extinguishment 4,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Modification.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" If the lender changes the terms, PSAK 109 asks whether they are ","type":"text"},{"text":"substantially different","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": whether the present value of the new cash flows, including fees, discounted at the ","type":"text"},{"text":"original","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" EIR, differs by at least 10 percent from the present value of the remaining original cash flows, which at the original EIR is the carrying amount (para. B3.3.6). For example (illustration), a 100,000 loan at 6 percent with three years left is renegotiated in two ways.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A: five years at 5%, fee 500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"B: principal cut to 85,000, five years at 4%","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PV of new flows at 6%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"96,287.64","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"77,838.98","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Change","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3.71%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"22.16%","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Result","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Modification","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Extinguishment","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accounting","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Remeasure at 6%: 95,787.64; gain 4,212.36; fee 500 reduces the carrying amount","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Derecognise; new loan at fair value at the market rate of 8%: 71,424.79","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Journal","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Bank loans 4,212.36 / Cr Gain on modification 4,212.36; Dr Bank loans 500 / Cr Cash 500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Bank loans (old) 100,000 / Cr Bank loans (new) 71,424.79 / Cr Gain on extinguishment 28,575.21","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 10 percent test is a floor, not the whole test. A change that alters the instrument's nature, such as a new currency or an added equity conversion feature, is usually treated as substantially different even below 10 percent (practice, STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED). For example (illustration), in a restructuring that takes route B, Morgan National Bank cuts a 10,500,000 note to 9,000,000 at 8 percent for five years, and the new note at the 15 percent market rate is worth 6,837,779, a gain of 3,662,221. PHE's policy follows PSAK 109 (Note 3g, Daftar 5/47, PDF p. 64).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Benchmark reform.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" When LIBOR ended in 2023, loans moved to SOFR plus a ","type":"text"},{"text":"credit adjustment spread","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (CAS). PSAK 109 lets a change required by the reform, on an economically equivalent basis, be treated as a reset of a floating rate: the EIR is updated and no gain or loss arises (paras. 5.4.7–5.4.9). PHE's project loan for Jambaran-Tiung Biru moved from LIBOR + 2.95 percent to SOFR + CAS + 2.95 percent on Tranche A, and from LIBOR + 2.15 percent to SOFR + CAS + 2.15 percent on Tranche B, after September 2023 (Note 27, Daftar 5/132, PDF p. 149).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. The fair value option and off-balance-sheet financing","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A borrower may designate a liability at fair value through profit or loss to remove an accounting mismatch (para. 4.2.2). The change in fair value due to the borrower's own credit risk then goes to other comprehensive income (OCI), not profit (para. 5.7.7). Own-credit amounts in OCI are never recycled to profit. For example (illustration, the split is this page's), a note of 500 falls to 480, 15 of it from the issuer's weaker credit and 5 from higher market rates.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Notes payable 20 / Cr OCI 15 / Cr Gain in profit or loss 5","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Had the whole fall come from credit risk, all 20 would go to OCI under PSAK 109. PHE carries all its borrowings at amortised cost (Note 3g, PDF p. 61).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Off-balance-sheet financing","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" keeps debt out of the borrower's statements, through unconsolidated project companies or special purpose entities. Owning less than 50 percent does not by itself keep a subsidiary out: under PSAK 110 (formerly PSAK 65, IFRS 10) control decides, whatever the shareholding. A ","type":"text"},{"text":"financial guarantee","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" the parent gives a lender is recognised at fair value and then carried at the higher of the expected credit loss and the unamortised initial amount (PSAK 109 para. 4.2.1(c)); a ","type":"text"},{"text":"comfort letter","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" that is not a guarantee is not recognised. PIEP gave one in 2017 for its subsidiary M&P's syndicated loan and states it \"shall not be construed as acting as a guarantor\" (Note 27, Daftar 5/134, PDF p. 151). Because M&P is consolidated, its loan is already in PHE's borrowings; the letter matters only in PIEP's own accounts.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. Sukuk: the issuer's side","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A ","type":"text"},{"text":"sukuk","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is a sharia security representing an undivided share in assets, in the benefit of assets, or in an investment activity. The holder earns a share of rent or profit rather than interest. Indonesia's issuer accounting sits in PSAK 410 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Akuntansi Sukuk","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 110; the new PSAK 110 is consolidation, a renumbering trap) and covers two forms.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Sukuk ijarah","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Sukuk mudharabah","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"What the holder owns","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Benefit of a leased asset; return is rent (","type":"text"},{"text":"ijarah","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" fee)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Share of a business venture; return is a profit share (","type":"text"},{"text":"nisbah","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":")","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Initial measurement","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Fair value (nominal adjusted for premium or discount), less transaction costs","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Nominal; transaction costs a separate deferred charge","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Afterwards","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Costs and premium or discount amortised straight-line as sukuk issuance expense; ijarah fee expensed when due","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Costs amortised straight-line; the holders' share is a distribution of revenue, not an expense","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Presentation","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Liability, net of unamortised costs","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Temporary syirkah funds, between liabilities and equity (last among liabilities if the entity has no such section)","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Both forms spread costs straight-line, where PSAK 109 would use the effective interest rate. PHE's own policy lists the sukuk payable among loans and borrowings at amortised cost under the EIR method (Note 3g, PDF pp. 61 and 63), and never cites PSAK 410.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's sukuk payable is a sukuk ijarah issued by its subsidiary Elnusa on 11 August 2020: Rp700,000 million, five years, an ijarah fee of Rp63,000 million a year (9 percent) paid quarterly, maturing 11 August 2025 (Note 22, Daftar 5/116–5/117, PDF pp. 133–134). For example (illustration), translate it at one constant rate, so nominal 43,325 and issuance costs 224 as in Note 22.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"On issue: Dr Cash 43,101 / Cr Sukuk ijarah payable 43,101","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Each quarter: Dr Ijarah expense 974.81 / Cr Cash 974.81, where 974.81 = 43,325 × 9% / 4","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Each year: Dr Sukuk issuance expense 44.80 / Cr Sukuk ijarah payable 44.80, where 44.80 = 224 / 5","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 22 shows the net: 43,325 − 224 + 188 = 43,289. Islamic tranches also appear inside PHE's bank loans: a ","type":"text"},{"text":"wakala","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (agency-based) tranche of the Jambaran-Tiung Biru financing and a Bank Syariah Indonesia facility to an Elnusa subsidiary. PHE reports both as bank loans; the wakala tranche is priced off SOFR like the conventional tranches (Note 27, PDF pp. 148 and 160).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. Borrowings across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Banks.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Borrowings, subordinated debt and deposits are all financial liabilities at amortised cost, but banks present the balance sheet in order of liquidity without a current/non-current split, which PSAK 201 allows when it is more relevant (para. 60). Maturity analysis then moves to the notes.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Power, toll roads and other project finance.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A project company borrows ","type":"text"},{"text":"non-recourse","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": lenders are repaid only from the project's cash flows. Covenants centre on the ","type":"text"},{"text":"debt service coverage ratio","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (DSCR), cash flow available for debt service divided by principal and interest due. For example (illustration), cash available 13,000 against debt service of 10,000 gives 1.30 against a covenant of 1.20. Lenders often require a ","type":"text"},{"text":"debt service reserve account","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", which the borrower shows as restricted cash (see the Restricted cash essay). Interest during construction is capitalised under PSAK 223 (see the Fixed assets essay).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Mining and plantations.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Many Indonesian commodity exporters borrow in USD because they sell in USD, a natural hedge. Many also have the US dollar as functional currency (PSAK 221, IAS 21), as PHE does, so the loan creates no exchange differences. A company whose functional currency is the rupiah carries the gains and losses through profit unless it designates a hedge.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"State-owned enterprises.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Funds on-lent by the government through ","type":"text"},{"text":"subsidiary loan agreements","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" are borrowings. Any below-market element is a grant under PSAK 220 when the government acts as lender or regulator; when it lends as the SOE's shareholder, the logic of a parent's loan (section 4) points to a capital contribution. Which applies is a judgement on the facts.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Notes 26, 27, 22, 44h and 50","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 107 (IFRS 7) asks for carrying amounts, terms, a maturity analysis, interest, defaults and breaches. Note 27 gives each facility by borrower (Daftar 5/130, PDF p. 147).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Borrower","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Current 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Non-current 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total 2023","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE (the Company)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,062,320","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"183,040","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,245,360","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,067,680","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PEPC (Jambaran-Tiung Biru)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"189,212","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,019,207","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,208,419","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,386,663","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHI","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"166,800","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"249,600","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"416,400","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"583,200","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PEP","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"88,960","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"133,120","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"222,080","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"649,440","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PIEP (M&P)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"39,561","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"64,900","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"104,461","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"146,467","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PDSI and Elnusa (rupiah)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5,261","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"30,664","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"35,925","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"14,250","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gross principal","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,552,114","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,680,531","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,232,645","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4,847,700","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Less arrangement fees","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(1,080)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(4,636)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(5,716)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(14,243)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Long-term bank loans","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,551,034","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,675,895","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,226,929","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4,833,457","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 50 reconciles all borrowings, including sukuk and the shareholder loan (Daftar 5/235, PDF p. 252).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4,978,575 + drawdowns 56,412 − repayments 1,679,292 + non-cash 5,171 = 3,360,866","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"= short-term 15,775 + long-term 3,226,929 + shareholder loan 74,873 + sukuk 43,289","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Share due within one year = 1,551,034 / 3,226,929 = 48.1%","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Implied cost of borrowing = interest on loans 297,328 (Note 41) / average borrowings ((4,978,575 + 3,360,866) / 2 = 4,169,721) = 7.1%, approximate, since the numerator is after 2,277 of interest capitalised (Note 12, PDF p. 126)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net debt = 3,360,866 − cash 2,614,318 = 746,548; against total equity 15,107,352 = 0.05","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The last ratio is only a proxy for the covenant, whose definitions of net debt and tangible net worth are not disclosed, but it suggests wide headroom against 2.00. PHE states it complied with all covenants (Note 27). Almost half the line falls due in 2025, mainly the 940,000 bullet. PHE discloses no refinancing of it at the reporting date, and it held cash of 2,614,318. Note 27 shows 560,000 undrawn under the 2023 agreement, without saying whether it was still available (PDF p. 157). The USD 200 million six-month loan PHE took in February 2025 (Note 52c, Daftar 5/237, PDF p. 254) came from PAEP, a wholly owned subsidiary (Note 1), so it leaves consolidated borrowings unchanged; an external loan signed then would not have reclassified the bullet either (para. 72).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Two disclosures do not follow the rules taught above.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The sukuk matures on 11 August 2025, within twelve months, yet it is presented as non-current on the balance sheet and in the 1–5 year column of the liquidity table (Note 49, Daftar 5/233, PDF p. 250). Under section 5 it should be current.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The liquidity table's \"bank and shareholder loans\" due within a year, 1,566,809, equals long-term current maturities plus short-term loans (1,551,034 + 15,775) and leaves out the 74,873 shareholder loan, as does the 2023 column. It also shows carrying amounts, where PSAK 107 asks for undiscounted contractual cash flows.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11. Upstream note: project finance at Jambaran-Tiung Biru","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PEPC financed the Jambaran-Tiung Biru gas development through a ","type":"text"},{"text":"trustee borrowing structure","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": facilities of 1,679,940 after a 166,460 cancellation. The 122,859 remaining in the trustee accounts is carried as restricted cash (Note 27, PDF pp. 148–150). Half of each tranche's notional is swapped from the floating benchmark to fixed base rates of 1.79–1.99 percent (315,000 of 630,000; 479,970 of 959,940), so half the notional was hedged at the effective date; the swaps' amortisation profile is not disclosed. Tranches amortise semi-annually to 2029 and 2034, the longest-dated part of the line.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12. Related lines","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"long-term-bank-loans-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"text":"short-term-bank-loans","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"text":"other-loans-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"text":"sukuk-payables","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"text":": the rest of PHE's borrowings, covered on this page.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"cash-and-cash-equivalents","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/cash-and-cash-equivalents","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"text":"restricted-cash","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/restricted-cash","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the other side of net debt and the trustee account.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"finance-cost","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/finance-cost","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": interest at the effective rate.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"fixed-assets-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/fixed-assets-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": capitalised borrowing costs.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"lease-liabilities-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/right-of-use-assets-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the other interest-bearing debt (see the Right-of-use assets essay).","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That PEP prepaid about 277,000 of its facility A2 in 2024 is inferred from Note 27 and Note 50; PHE does not describe it, and the 8,527 fall in unamortised fees is consistent with but does not prove a write-off.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE appears to measure the sukuk ijarah under PSAK 109 rather than PSAK 410. Its policy puts the sukuk among loans at amortised cost by the EIR method, and the amortised costs (83.9 and 61.7 percent of the total at the end of 2024 and 2023) fit an effective-interest pattern (about 82–88 and 60–65 percent) rather than straight line (87.8 and 67.7 percent). With costs of 224 on 43,325 the difference is immaterial. The sukuk is also shown as non-current although it matures in August 2025.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The PSAK 410 treatment in section 8 is from an IAI teaching deck that disclaims being IAI's official view. The standard's text was not opened.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The covenant proxy of 0.05 uses total equity and reported cash. The agreements' definitions are not disclosed.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Other PHE slips not used in the body: Note 27's facility A1 total reads 943,480,000 for 943,840,000 (PDF p. 155); facility interest (299,827 with the shareholder loan) exceeds Note 41's 297,328 by 2,499, mostly the 2,277 capitalised (Note 12), with 222 and the location of the sukuk fee and M&P's interest undisclosed. The industry notes in section 9 are general practice, not checked against named companies.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1–1/3, PDF pp. 11–13); Note 1 (subsidiaries); Note 12 (PDF p. 126); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 3b (Daftar 5/30, PDF p. 47); Note 3g (Daftar 5/44–5/47, PDF pp. 61–64); Note 22 (Daftar 5/116–5/117, PDF pp. 133–134); Note 26 (Daftar 5/125, PDF p. 142); Note 27 (Daftar 5/130–5/143, PDF pp. 147–160); Note 41 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167); Note 44h–i (Daftar 5/187, PDF p. 204); Note 47 (Daftar 5/222–5/224, PDF pp. 239–241); Note 49 liquidity table (Daftar 5/233, PDF p. 250); Note 50 (Daftar 5/235, PDF p. 252); Note 52c (Daftar 5/237, PDF p. 254). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 109 (IFRS 9), paras. 3.3.1, 3.3.3, 4.2.1(c), 4.2.2, 5.1.1, 5.4.7–5.4.9, 5.7.7, B3.3.6, B5.4.5: 10 percent test VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation agenda papers; IFRIC update Nov 2016 via IAS Plus); other paragraphs STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 201 (IAS 1), paras. 60, 69, 72–75; 2022 amendment ","type":"text"},{"text":"Non-current Liabilities with Covenants","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", paras. 72B, 76ZA, effective 1 January 2024: amendment content VERIFIED (web, BDO IFR Bulletin 2022/07; EY Developments 209); paras. 60, 69, 72–75 STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 410 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Akuntansi Sukuk","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 110): number VERIFIED (web, IAI, ","type":"text"},{"text":"DSAS Terkini","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" XV 2023; Binus Accounting, 2024). Issuer measurement and presentation VERIFIED (web, IAI, \"PSAK Syariah for Academics – Akuntansi Sukuk\", 28 Feb 2024, slides 36–40, speaker's personal views; fetched again at recheck).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"ISAK 119 (IFRIC 19), PSAK 220 (IAS 20) para. 10A, PSAK 221 (IAS 21), PSAK 110 (IFRS 10), PSAK 107 (IFRS 7), PSAK 223 (IAS 23): numbers from the IAI renumbering rule (IFRS n → PSAK 100+n, IAS n → PSAK 200+n, IFRIC n → ISAK 100+n); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intermediate Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", IFRS edition, ch. 14. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"The theory of long-term borrowings from pricing to derecognition, worked through PHE's syndicated loans and a subsidiary's sukuk ijarah, and set beside the industries that borrow differently","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"key_takeaways":["A loan is recognised at the cash actually received, net of the costs of arranging it, and then carried at amortised cost. The effective interest rate spreads coupons, fees, discounts and premiums over the loan's life as one interest expense, so the carrying amount climbs or falls to the amount repayable.","The current/non-current split turns on one question, asked at the reporting date: does the borrower have a right to defer settlement for at least twelve months? Since the 2024 amendments to PSAK 201, only covenants tested on or before that date can take the right away; later covenants are disclosed.","A loan leaves the balance sheet when it is repaid, extinguished or modified so much that it is in substance a new loan (the 10 percent test). Sukuk follow their own standard, PSAK 410: a sukuk ijarah is a liability with costs amortised straight-line, and a sukuk mudharabah is temporary syirkah funds, outside liabilities."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = 'e7716f4d-6d57-443d-9221-92b00916bbd7' AND updated_at = '2026-10-12T09:47:08.318531+00:00'::timestamptz;
